        "### Concept of arrays"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "BKad3aNVvDFm"
      },
      "source": [
        "Before writing code, let us understand **what an array is**, how it is stored in the computer's\n",
        "memory, and when arrays help and when they get in the way. This theory holds in almost every\n",
        "programming language (C, C#, Java, Python), so it will keep paying off in later courses."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "-39PjVh0vDFn"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### What is an array?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "KGooj6VJvDFo"
      },
      "source": [
        "An **array** is a data structure that stores **a collection of values of the same kind** under\n",
        "**one name**, kept **in order**, where each value is reached by its **position number**, called the\n",
        "**index**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "1Gr2hMHXvDFp"
      },
      "source": [
        "> **Short definition**\n",
        ">\n",
        "> Array = one name + many elements of one kind + a fixed order + access by index."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "45xMzevgvDFq"
      },
      "source": [
        "| Term             | Meaning                                                  | Example for `temp = [28, 31, 30]` |\n",
        "| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n",
        "| **Element**      | One value inside the array                               | `28`, `31`, `30`                  |\n",
        "| **Index**        | The element's position number, starting at **0**         | `31` is at index `1`              |\n",
        "| **Length**       | How many elements there are                              | `len(temp)` = `3`                 |\n",
        "| **Element type** | The kind of data of all elements (ideally the same)      | whole numbers (°C)                |\n",
        "| **Dimension**    | How many indices point to one element (1D row, 2D table) | 1-dimensional                     |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "L7bGuRd2vDFr"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Arrays around us"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "V9BVk9W5vDFr"
      },
      "source": [
        "- **Class scores**: 30 exam scores, one per student.\n",
        "- **Sensor data**: turbine temperature logged every hour for 24 hours → an array of 24 elements.\n",
        "- **Energy production**: daily energy delivered over a month → an array of 30 elements (this week's\n",
        "  case study).\n",
        "- **Tables**: scores of 3 students × 3 exams → a **2-dimensional** array (rows and columns).\n",
        "- **Digital images**: pixels arranged in rows and columns → a 2-dimensional array of numbers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "vIikOe5hvDFs"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### How an array is stored in memory"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "zLV_r_RvvDFs"
      },
      "source": [
        "In a classic array (for example in C or C#), the elements are stored **next to each other** in\n",
        "memory. Suppose an array of 5 integers starts at address 1000 and each element takes 4 bytes:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Index          | 0    | 1    | 2    | 3    | 4    |\n",
        "| -------------- | ---- | ---- | ---- | ---- | ---- |\n",
        "| Memory address | 1000 | 1004 | 1008 | 1012 | 1016 |\n",
        "\n",
        "The computer does not search for an element; it simply **computes** its address:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_MsRBhC_vDFs"
      },
      "source": [
        "```text\n",
        "address of element i = start address + i × size of one element\n",
        "address of temp[3]   = 1000 + 3 × 4 = 1012\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This formula also explains **why the index starts at 0**: the index is the distance (offset) from\n",
        "the first element, and the first element is 0 steps from the start. A Python list works in a\n",
        "similar way: it stores a row of \"pointers\" to its values next to each other, so `a[i]` is fast too."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "dbBxpJZevDFt"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Advantages of arrays"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "hZq1s00-vDFt"
      },
      "source": [
        "1. **One name for many values.** 30 scores fit in `scores`, not `score1` to `score30`, so the code\n",
        "   is shorter and easier to read.\n",
        "2. **Direct access by index.** Reading or changing `a[i]` is just as fast for the first element as\n",
        "   for the 10,000th, because the address is computed, not searched for.\n",
        "3. **Made for loops.** Totals, averages, maximums, searches, and charts all use the same loop, however\n",
        "   many values there are.\n",
        "4. **Tidy and efficient in memory.** Elements that sit next to each other are read quickly by the\n",
        "   processor.\n",
        "5. **The basis of other data structures.** Strings, tables (2D lists), images, signals, NumPy arrays,\n",
        "   and Pandas DataFrames (Week 8) are all built on the array idea."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "tBdZlq8EvDFt"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Disadvantages of arrays"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "lsH23OK6vDFu"
      },
      "source": [
        "1. **Fixed size (in classic arrays).** In C, C#, or Java the size is set when the array is created.\n",
        "   Too large wastes memory; too small means the data does not fit.\n",
        "2. **Inserting or removing in the middle is slow.** Because the elements must stay side by side,\n",
        "   every element after that point has to be **shifted** one by one. Inserting at the front of a list\n",
        "   of 1 million elements shifts 1 million elements.\n",
        "3. **One kind of data only (in classic arrays).** All elements must have the same type; mixing names,\n",
        "   numbers, and dates in one array makes processing hard.\n",
        "4. **Finding a value without knowing its position means checking one by one** (linear search),\n",
        "   unless the data is sorted.\n",
        "5. **An index out of range is an error.** Python stops with `IndexError`; in languages such as C a\n",
        "   program may even read random memory without any warning.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operation on an array / list                | Speed               | Why                                    |\n",
        "| ------------------------------------------- | ------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n",
        "| Reading or changing `a[i]`                  | Fast                | The address is computed from the index |\n",
        "| Adding at the end (`append`)                | Usually fast        | No element needs to move               |\n",
        "| Inserting / removing at the front or middle | Slow for large data | Later elements must be shifted         |\n",
        "| Finding a value (`x in a`)                  | Slow for large data | Checked one by one                     |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "UZCJfHkcvDFu"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Classic arrays and Python lists"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "qKSmKUj5vDFu"
      },
      "source": [
        "Python has no built-in \"array\" type like C or C#; a **list** takes that role and is more flexible.\n",
        "This removes some of the disadvantages of arrays, but not all of them.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Property                | Classic array (C, C#, Java) | Python list                              |\n",
        "| ----------------------- | --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n",
        "| Size                    | Fixed when created          | Can grow and shrink (`append`, `pop`)    |\n",
        "| Element type            | Must be the same            | May be mixed (in this course: one kind)  |\n",
        "| First index             | 0                           | 0                                        |\n",
        "| Access `a[i]`           | Fast                        | Fast                                     |\n",
        "| Index from the end      | Not in C/Java               | Yes: `a[-1]` is the last element         |\n",
        "| Insert/remove in middle | Slow (must shift)           | Still slow for large lists (also shifts) |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ChTtVI4DvDFv"
      },
      "source": [
        "> **Discussion 6: Discuss: a good fit for an array or not?**\n",
