          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# one_list.py\n",
        "# With an array (a Python list): one name for all the scores\n",
        "scores = [80, 65, 90, 75, 70]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Average:\", sum(scores) / len(scores))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "tCP0vgdSvDFx"
      },
      "source": [
        "The second program does not care whether there are 5 scores or 500: only the list changes, the calculation stays the same."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "D-iMuukEvDFx"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### An array is a row of numbered boxes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Om2OvHYYvDFy"
      },
      "source": [
        "Picture a shelf with numbered boxes. The shelf has a **name** (`scores`), every box holds **one value**, and every box has a **position number** called the **index**.\n",
        "\n",
        "| index | 0   | 1   | 2   | 3   | 4   |\n",
        "| ----- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n",
        "| value | 80  | 65  | 90  | 75  | 70  |\n",
        "\n",
        "An array has four properties:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Property         | Meaning                                                           |\n",
        "| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n",
        "| **One name**     | All values are reached through the same name, here `scores`.      |\n",
        "| **Ordered**      | The boxes keep their order: box 2 is always the third value.      |\n",
        "| **Index from 0** | The first box is index `0`, the last of 5 boxes is index `4`.     |\n",
        "| **Same kind**    | All values mean the same kind of thing (all scores, all °C, ...). |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "nQAURwMPvDFy"
      },
      "source": [
        "> **Why does counting start at 0?**\n",
        ">\n",
        "> The index tells how many boxes you skip from the start. The first box needs no skipping, so its\n",
        "> index is `0`. An array with 5 elements has indices 0 to 4, so the last index is always `len - 1`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "PrXJGDqwvDFz"
      },
      "source": [
        "In Python, the **list** plays the role of an array. A Python list can also grow and shrink, and it _can_ mix kinds, but in this course we keep each list to one kind of value. (In Week 8 you will meet NumPy arrays, which are much faster for big numeric data.)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "wfur0tgLvDFz"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Arrays with Python lists"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "yaYJLLIbvDFz"
      },
      "source": [
