      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "22bboMD3vDF9"
      },
      "source": [
        "Before writing loops yourself, get to know Python's built-in tools that work on **a whole array at once**. Inside, Python still checks the elements one by one, but you do not have to write it. The data is now **bigger**: 24 values, then 30 values. Every block continues the previous one; the data is repeated in each block so that it runs on its own.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Question                     | Tool (no loop)                    | Result                          |\n",
        "| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |\n",
        "| How many values?             | `len(a)`                          | a whole number                  |\n",
        "| Total and average?           | `sum(a)`, `sum(a) / len(a)`       | a number                        |\n",
        "| Highest and lowest?          | `max(a)`, `min(a)`                | an element value                |\n",
        "| At which position?           | `a.index(x)`                      | the **first** index holding `x` |\n",
        "| How many times? Is it there? | `a.count(x)`, `x in a`            | a count, `True`/`False`         |\n",
        "| Part of the data?            | `a[start:stop:step]`              | a **new** list                  |\n",
        "| Sorted?                      | `sorted(a)`, `a.sort()`           | a new list / the list itself    |\n",
        "| A list without typing it?    | `list(range(1, 31))`, `[24] * 30` | a new list                      |"
      ]
    },
