    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "CWBj7-79vDGM"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Loops and conditions with arrays"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "aUT8s4-evDGM"
      },
      "source": [
        "Built-in functions answer the common questions: total, highest, position, order. But many questions have **their own condition**: which days were below target? when did the value drop sharply from the day before? For those we write our own **loop** (`for`) and decide with a **condition** (`if`). We go back to tiny data first so every step can be followed by eye, then apply it to the 30-day data.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Sequential processing** means visiting the elements **one at a time, in order, from index 0 to the last**, while keeping a running result. Almost every analysis of data in a list uses this pattern, so we practise it on tiny data first."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "KhYnG5WPvDGM"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Looping over a list: two ways"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "O9-_T6PrvDGN"
      },
      "source": [
        "The **for-each** loop hands you each _value_. The **by-position** loop hands you each _index_ `i`, so you can also reach neighbours and know where you are. `range(5)` produces 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, so `range(len(scores))` produces exactly the valid indices."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 22,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "k5K2xRkXvDGN",
        "outputId": "31d90c96-d474-48fb-c4df-0fccc48a7e26"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "range(5) gives: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\n",
            "score: 80\n",
            "score: 65\n",
            "score: 90\n",
            "score: 75\n",
            "score: 70\n",
            "index 0 holds 80\n",
            "index 1 holds 65\n",
            "index 2 holds 90\n",
            "index 3 holds 75\n",
            "index 4 holds 70\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# loop_two_ways.py\n",
        "scores = [80, 65, 90, 75, 70]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"range(5) gives:\", list(range(5)))\n",
        "\n",
        "# 1) for-each: take each VALUE in turn\n",
        "for score in scores:\n",
        "    print(\"score:\", score)\n",
        "\n",
        "# 2) by position: i is the INDEX, scores[i] is the value\n",
        "for i in range(len(scores)):\n",
        "    print(\"index\", i, \"holds\", scores[i])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "KEy-xMOMvDGO"
      },
      "source": [
        "> **Which one to use?**\n",
        ">\n",
        "> Use **for-each** when you only need the values. Use **range(len(...))** when you need the position\n",
        "> too: for the day number, for comparing with the previous element, or for changing elements."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "7SNZzjdLvDGO"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### The accumulator pattern: total, average, count"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Q0GA2jNZvDGO"
      },
      "source": [
        "An **accumulator** is a variable that starts at a neutral value (0) and is updated at every step of the loop. After the loop it holds the answer.\n",
        "\n",
        "| step  | element | running total |\n",
        "| ----- | ------- | ------------- |\n",
        "| start | -       | 0             |\n",
        "| 1     | 80      | 80            |\n",
        "| 2     | 65      | 145           |\n",
        "| 3     | 90      | 235           |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "esdN7EGzvDGP"
      },
      "source": [
        "> **Activity 6F: Predict, then run**\n",
        ">\n",
        "> Continue the table by hand for the last two elements (75 and 70). What are the final total and the\n",
        "> average? How many scores are 75 or higher? Then run the block."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 23,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "G2uLNd2pvDGP",
        "outputId": "641526ec-d6fc-45c4-b2b1-14f191aa289d"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "running total: 80\n",
            "running total: 145\n",
            "running total: 235\n",
            "running total: 310\n",
            "running total: 380\n",
            "Total   : 380\n",
            "Average : 76.0\n",
            "Scores >= 75: 3 of 5\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# accumulator.py\n",
        "scores = [80, 65, 90, 75, 70]\n",
        "\n",
        "total = 0                    # accumulator: starts at zero\n",
        "count_pass = 0               # a counter is an accumulator that adds 1\n",
        "for score in scores:\n",
        "    total = total + score    # add each element\n",
        "    if score >= 75:\n",
        "        count_pass = count_pass + 1\n",
        "    print(\"running total:\", total)\n",
        "\n",
        "average = total / len(scores)\n",
        "print(\"Total   :\", total)\n",
        "print(\"Average :\", average)\n",
        "print(\"Scores >= 75:\", count_pass, \"of\", len(scores))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ZndVwF-mvDGQ"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Highest and lowest value"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ur7HMXcbvDGQ"
      },
      "source": [
        "Start by assuming the **first element** is the best so far. Compare every element with it; when you find a better one, it becomes the new \"best so far\"."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 24,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "88Co8qH5vDGQ",
        "outputId": "a49d74d8-33e3-46e9-caed-847c0981b665"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Highest: 90\n",
            "Lowest : 65\n",
            "Built-in check: 90 65\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# highest_lowest.py\n",
        "scores = [80, 65, 90, 75, 70]\n",
        "\n",
        "highest = scores[0]          # best so far (assume the first one)\n",
        "lowest = scores[0]\n",
        "for score in scores:\n",
        "    if score > highest:\n",
        "        highest = score\n",
        "    if score < lowest:\n",
        "        lowest = score\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Highest:\", highest)\n",
        "print(\"Lowest :\", lowest)\n",
        "print(\"Built-in check:\", max(scores), min(scores))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "aet2PHlZvDGR"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Not only the value, but the position"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_cYqdYM1vDGR"
      },
      "source": [
        "`max()` tells you the highest temperature but not **which day** it was. To get that, remember the **index** of the best element instead of its value; the value is then `temps[pos_max]`.\n",
        "\n",
        "| index | 0   | 1   | 2   | 3   | 4   |\n",
        "| ----- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n",
        "| temps | 28  | 31  | 30  | 33  | 29  |\n",
        "\n",
        "The highest value, 33, sits at index 3, which is day 4."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 25,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "g_3Vf77vvDGR",
        "outputId": "7cf7f68e-adbe-4d25-e2ce-cf0bee66ea04"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Hottest value: 33 °C\n",
            "Found at index 3 = day 4\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# position_of_max.py\n",
        "temps = [28, 31, 30, 33, 29]    # temperature (°C), day 1 to day 5\n",
        "\n",
        "pos_max = 0                      # position of the best so far\n",
        "for i in range(len(temps)):\n",
        "    if temps[i] > temps[pos_max]:\n",
        "        pos_max = i              # keep the POSITION, not just the value\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Hottest value:\", temps[pos_max], \"°C\")\n",
        "print(\"Found at index\", pos_max, \"= day\", pos_max + 1)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "1heSX6k9vDGS"
      },
      "source": [
        "> **Index and day number**\n",
        ">\n",
        "> Day _n_ is at index `n - 1`. When you print for people, show `i + 1`. If two elements tie for the\n",
        "> highest value, `>` keeps the first one; `>=` would keep the last one."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "jIM65_A2vDGS"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Linear search: stop at the first match"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "MG9x1H6XvDGT"
      },
      "source": [
        "A **linear search** checks the elements one by one and stops with `break` at the first one that matches, so the rest need not be checked. Keep the **index** where you stopped, or `None` if nothing matched."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 26,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "kUXjSUY5vDGT",
        "outputId": "d7765037-4fe4-4416-d35b-991f5108b250"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "First day above 32 °C: index 3\n",
            "Elements checked: 4 of 5\n",
            "True\n",
            "3\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# linear_search.py\n",
        "temps = [28, 31, 30, 33, 29]\n",
        "target = 32                       # first day hotter than 32 °C?\n",
        "\n",
        "found = None                      # None = \"nothing found (yet)\"\n",
        "checks = 0\n",
        "for i in range(len(temps)):\n",
        "    checks = checks + 1\n",
        "    if temps[i] > target:\n",
        "        found = i\n",
        "        break                     # stop at the first match\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"First day above\", target, \"°C: index\", found)\n",
        "print(\"Elements checked:\", checks, \"of\", len(temps))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Handy shortcuts\n",
        "print(33 in temps)                # is 33 somewhere in the list?\n",
        "print(temps.index(33))            # position of the first 33 (error if absent)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Zz2jj5dZvDGU"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Filtering: a new list of matching elements"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "m-SM6x8YvDGU"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Filtering** builds a **new list** that contains only the elements meeting a condition. Start from an empty list and `append` each match."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 27,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "51I6ZziUvDGV",
        "outputId": "67192c57-f0be-4047-828c-b3dcec692146"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Hot values  : [31, 30, 33]\n",
            "How many    : 3\n",
            "Hot days    : [2, 3, 4]\n",
            "Same result? True\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# filtering.py\n",
        "temps = [28, 31, 30, 33, 29]\n",
        "\n",
        "hot = []                          # the new list starts empty\n",
        "for t in temps:\n",
        "    if t >= 30:\n",
        "        hot.append(t)\n",
        "print(\"Hot values  :\", hot)\n",
        "print(\"How many    :\", len(hot))\n",
        "\n",
        "# To keep the day numbers instead of the values, loop by position\n",
        "hot_days = []\n",
        "for i in range(len(temps)):\n",
        "    if temps[i] >= 30:\n",
        "        hot_days.append(i + 1)\n",
        "print(\"Hot days    :\", hot_days)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Optional: the same filter as a list comprehension (a compact loop)\n",
        "hot_again = [t for t in temps if t >= 30]\n",
        "print(\"Same result?\", hot_again == hot)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "C30WEIh3vDGV"
      },
      "source": [
        "`[expression for item in list if condition]` is a **list comprehension**. It is optional in this course: it gives exactly the same result as the loop with `append` above it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Z8X3EaH8vDGW"
      },
      "source": [
        "#### Neighbours: daily change and moving average"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "SQyUMz7KvDGW"
      },
      "source": [
        "Many questions compare an element with the one **before** it (how much did it change?) or with its neighbours (what is the average of the last 3 days?). We need the index `i` for that. Here are 7 days of temperature:\n",
        "\n",
        "| index | 0   | 1   | 2   | 3   | 4   | 5   | 6   |\n",
        "| ----- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n",
        "| temps | 28  | 31  | 30  | 33  | 29  | 27  | 32  |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Daily change**: today minus yesterday. Day 1 has no yesterday, so the loop starts at index **1**.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Moving average**: the average of a window that slides one step at a time. A 3-day window ending at index 2 covers indices 0, 1, 2; the next covers 1, 2, 3; and so on. 7 values give 7 - 3 + 1 = **5** averages."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 28,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "LvD_GoyYvDGX",
        "outputId": "2a6d7542-e9c4-450f-cec8-5314cbdb315b"
      },
      "outputs": [
